---
import FieldGuideLayout from '../../../components/FieldGuideLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<FieldGuideLayout
  title="Konami's custom extensions"
  description="Type tags inside RESO containers that aren't part of Nintendo's SDK. Konami added these for their own game data — widget tables, binary tables, and a few other things."
  crumbs={[
    { label: 'Field Guide', href: `${base}/field-guide/` },
    { label: 'File Formats', href: `${base}/field-guide/file-formats/` },
    { label: 'Konami extensions' },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    A <a href={`${base}/field-guide/file-formats/reso/`}>RESO container</a>
    entry carries a 4-character type tag. Most of those tags are Nintendo
    SDK formats (NCGR, NCLR, etc), but four of them are Konami's own:
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Tag in file</th><th>What it holds</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>tad</code></td><td>Static widget tables — fixed-stride records describing UI sprites and their positions.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>nib</code></td><td>"bin" backwards. Per-container binary data with a layout that varies by context.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>rnan</code></td><td>Standard NANR animation data, but Konami uses it as a generic container for some non-animation records too.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>sfo</code></td><td>A nested RESO container inside the current RESO entry.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>The <code>tad</code> table</h2>
  <p>
    The most useful Konami extension. A <code>tad</code> entry is a flat
    array of fixed-size records. Each record describes one widget on a
    UI screen — what sprite it is, where it goes, what palette and flags
    to use.
  </p>

  <p>
    In the buyitem container, the <code>tad</code> entry holds 76
    records of 28 bytes each (26 bytes of data plus a 2-byte
    <code>0xFFFF</code> terminator). Those records hold the parts list
    for the entire shop-buying UI.
  </p>

  <p>
    The bigger question — "where do these widgets actually get placed on
    screen?" — isn't answered by the <code>tad</code> table alone. The
    placement is in code, baked into one of the game's overlays. See
    <a href={`${base}/field-guide/2d-art/composition-recipes/`}>composition
    recipes</a> for the worked example.
  </p>

  <h2>The <code>nib</code> entries</h2>
  <p>
    Catch-all binary data. The layout depends on what the container is
    for. In the cooking-recipe container (<code>ucc/names.ofs</code>),
    the <code>nib</code> entry holds a 16-byte stride table of recipe
    metadata. In other containers it could be anything.
  </p>

  <p>
    When you see a <code>nib</code> entry, the next step is usually to
    look at the container's purpose and the size of the entry and try
    to spot a fixed-stride pattern. The records are almost always a
    multiple of some small power of two.
  </p>

  <h2>The <code>sfo</code> nested container</h2>
  <p>
    A RESO container inside a RESO container. The inner layout doesn't
    quite match the outer one — there's some additional framing that
    we haven't fully worked out. The clearest example is
    <code>global.lzs</code> entry 34, which is an <code>sfo</code> tag
    holding a list of sub-files. The inner table parses partially with
    the standard RESO reader but the entry layout has extra fields.
  </p>

  <p>
    For most uses we treat <code>sfo</code> as "compressed sub-blob" —
    extract the bytes, try to parse them as a RESO, and if that doesn't
    work, fall back to treating it as an opaque blob.
  </p>

  <h2>The <code>szl</code> tag</h2>
  <p>
    Not really a Konami extension — it's a fifth tag that's neither
    Nintendo SDK nor table data. It marks an LZ11-compressed sub-blob
    that, when decompressed, becomes another RESO container. So:
  </p>

  <pre><code>{`outer RESO entry (tag = szl)
  └─ LZ11 compressed bytes
      └─ inner RESO container
          └─ NCGR / NCLR / NCER ...`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    This is how the title screen pack stores its seasonal variants —
    one outer <code>szl</code> per variant, each one decompressing to
    a fresh inner RESO with the artwork.
  </p>
</FieldGuideLayout>
